---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { SITE } from '../data/site'
import { breadcrumbs, techArticle } from '../data/schema'

/* The palette, with the numbers that justify it. Every hex here is the one the
   app ships, generated from an OKLch source by the project's own
   `tools/palette.mjs`. */
const surfaces = [
  { token: '--bg-void', hex: '#040405', note: 'The stage. Everything floats on this.' },
  { token: '--bg-chrome', hex: '#0b0c0d', note: 'The top bar, which gives the window a top edge.' },
  { token: '--bg-island', hex: '#161719', note: 'A raised content surface.' },
  { token: '--bg-inset', hex: '#28282a', note: 'A field or control sunk into an island.' },
]
const ink = [
  { token: '--fg', hex: '#f6f7f9', note: 'Text, and the accent — they are the same value.' },
  { token: '--fg-mid', hex: '#b9babd', note: 'Secondary text.' },
  { token: '--fg-dim', hex: '#9a9c9f', note: 'Captions and metadata.' },
  { token: '--line', hex: '#2d2e30', note: 'A hairline between surfaces.' },
]
const states = [
  { token: '--st-working', hex: '#7bd77f', label: 'working' },
  { token: '--st-waiting', hex: '#efc845', label: 'waiting on you' },
  { token: '--st-error', hex: '#fb817a', label: 'stopped on an error' },
]
---

<Base
  title="True Ink — the design system behind cowork-deck"
  description="A near-black interface where hue belongs to state, elevation is lightness rather than shadow, and every colour pair is measured by a test that fails the build."
  schema={[
    techArticle({
      headline: 'True Ink — the design system behind cowork-deck',
      description:
        'Four decisions behind a dark interface for a window full of agent sessions: a nearly absent cool cast, elevation as lightness, light as the accent, and a terminal that deliberately does not follow the palette.',
      path: '/design/',
    }),
    breadcrumbs([
      { name: 'cowork-deck', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Design', path: '/design/' },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="phead">
      <p class="eyebrow">Design</p>
      <h1 class="h1--page">True Ink</h1>
      <p class="lede">
      A window holding twelve agent sessions has one job before it has any
      others: to say which of them needs a person. That constraint decided the
      whole palette, and it decided it in one sentence — <strong>hue belongs to
        state</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>1 · Hue belongs to state, so the accent is light</h2>
      <p>
        Green, amber and red mean working, waiting on you, and broken. They are
        never spent on decoration, which leaves the interface with no colour to
        be branded in — so the accent became light itself.
        <code>--accent</code> resolves to the same value as <code>--fg</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is not a collision waiting to be fixed. It has one visible
        consequence worth knowing before someone files it as a bug: a primary
        button is the ground and the ink inverted, so it reads as a switch that
        has been thrown rather than as a coloured call to action. For the
        actions this app has — <em>start</em>, <em>merge</em>, <em>close</em> —
        that is the right reading.
      </p>

      <div class="swatches" role="list">
        {
          states.map((s) => (
            <div class="swatch" role="listitem">
              <span class="swatch__chip" style={`background:${s.hex}`}></span>
              <code>{s.token}</code>
              <span class="swatch__hex">{s.hex}</span>
              <span class="swatch__note">{s.label}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h2>2 · Elevation is lightness, not shadow</h2>
      <p>
        <code>--bg-void</code> resolves to <code>#040405</code>. Four units of
        brightness remain below it and no amount of black can reach them — so on
        this ground a cast shadow is a declaration that does nothing. An earlier
        pass spent a 30px drop shadow at 75% black on every surface and got a
        flat interface whose stylesheet claimed otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        The step from stage to raised surface does that work instead:
        <strong>0.097 in L</strong>, twice what the previous ladder spent. The
        edge is paid for by a lit hairline along the top at 7.5% white, plus a
        two-pixel <em>contact</em> shadow — the one part of a shadow that still
        reads on near-black, because it is the line where an object meets what it
        lies on.
      </p>

      <div class="swatches" role="list">
        {
          surfaces.map((s) => (
            <div class="swatch" role="listitem">
              <span class="swatch__chip" style={`background:${s.hex}`}></span>
              <code>{s.token}</code>
              <span class="swatch__hex">{s.hex}</span>
              <span class="swatch__note">{s.note}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p>
        The three names carry the elevation itself, and the ladder is spaced by
        what has to be <em>distinguishable</em> rather than by even steps:
        <code>--bg-inset</code> sits 0.073 above <code>--bg-island</code>
        because a field is recognised by its fill, not by its border.
      </p>

      <div class="swatches" role="list">
        {
          ink.map((s) => (
            <div class="swatch" role="listitem">
              <span class="swatch__chip" style={`background:${s.hex}`}></span>
              <code>{s.token}</code>
              <span class="swatch__hex">{s.hex}</span>
              <span class="swatch__note">{s.note}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h2>3 · The cast is cool, and almost absent</h2>
      <p>
        OKLch hue 265 at chroma 0.003 — a near-black stage with a trace of cool
        in it. The pass before it used a warm graphite at hue ~70, which is a
        warm grey in theory and reads as <em>brown</em> in practice: beside
        <code>--st-working</code>'s green, a deck of twelve tiles picked up an
        earthy tint. A palette chosen to protect one signal had spent a cast
        competing with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Five directions — True Ink, Warm Ember, Graphite, Blue Steel, Deep
        Petrol — were authored in OKLch, resolved to sRGB, measured and drawn on
        identical fragments before one was picked. The other four are still in
        the generator, so reversing this decision means re-running a script
        rather than reopening an argument.
      </p>

      <h2>4 · The terminal does not follow the palette</h2>
      <p>
        A terminal is a window onto another program. Those six ANSI hues are
        Claude Code's, not the app's, and overriding them would be the app
        lying about somebody else's output. What the app owns is the
        <em>frame</em>: the background, the foreground, the cursor — and
        <code>brightBlack</code>, which is the terminal's equivalent of
        <code>--fg-dim</code> and is most of what Claude Code's secondary output
        is drawn in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The caret is the foreground rather than the accent. That stopped being a
        choice the moment the accent became light itself: of two names for the
        same value, only one is true of a terminal cursor.
      </p>

      <h2>Every colour pair is measured, and the test fails the build</h2>
      <p>
        <code>npm run contrast</code> reads the theme rather than quoting it,
        walks every pair the design claims, and fails if one falls under its
        threshold. Three pairs deliberately do fall under, and the script
        documents which three and why — a documented exception is a decision, an
        undocumented one is a bug nobody has found yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The diff is where this pays off most visibly. Colour is the third
        channel there, never the channel: the added and removed bands measure
        roughly 1.0 against each other, so the literal <code>+</code> and
        <code>−</code> in their own column do the work. The diff reads for
        someone who cannot tell the two bands apart, which is a larger number of
        people than most diff views assume.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img
          src="/img/pull-requests.webp"
          width="1600"
          height="857"
          alt="A pull request's diff in True Ink: two sticky line-number columns, plus and minus markers in a column of their own, and added and removed bands so close in lightness that the markers carry the meaning."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The two bands measure ~1.0 against each other. What tells you which
          line is which is the character, not the colour.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The reasoning, in full</h2>
      <p>
        The complete record — the OKLch source, the generator, the five
        directions on identical fragments, the mockups, the mapping table
        between the mockups' token names and the app's, and the measurements —
        is in the repository under
        <a
          href={`${SITE.repo}/tree/main/docs/design/true-ink`}
          rel="noopener">docs/design/true-ink</a
        >. The mockups need no build step; open them in a browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site uses the same tokens, copied rather than imported: a landing
        page that had drifted a shade from the product it shows would be worse
        than one that never matched.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
